# cmesh BYOL hardening — sshguard never-block list (installed as /etc/sshguard/whitelist)
#
# Addresses listed here are never blocked by sshguard, whatever they do. Put your own
# management egress here — office, VPN pool, bastion — so a client that fails SSH
# authentication in a loop cannot lock you out of the host.
#
# Why this matters: a failed public-key attempt scores 10 and THRESHOLD is 30, so three
# of them inside DETECTION_TIME earn a 2-minute ban. Repeat bans double, and 120 points
# of history is a permanent entry in /var/db/sshguard/blacklist.db, which sshguard never
# lifts, even across restarts. The ban is keyed on the source address, so one bad script
# behind a NAT locks out everyone behind the same address.
#
# One address, CIDR block, or hostname per line; '#' starts a comment. Hostnames are
# resolved once, at sshguard startup. cmesh-byol-harden seeds this file only when it is
# absent, so edits here survive a re-run.
#
# Examples — replace with your own and uncomment:
#   203.0.113.7/32        # office egress
#   198.51.100.0/24       # VPN pool
